% ============================================================
%  CHAPTER 1: PROJECT SYNOPSIS
% ============================================================
\chapter{Project Synopsis}

\section{Executive Summary}

\textbf{SentinelShark} is a high-performance, modern desktop \textbf{Network Intrusion Detection and Analysis System (NIDS)}, engineered as an intelligent, real-time threat-correlated alternative to classic packet analyzers such as Wireshark. While conventional sniffers provide raw packet inspection, they leave security analysts with the cumbersome manual burden of extracting IP addresses and looking them up across disparate reputation feeds. SentinelShark bridges this gap by unifying live packet capture, deep protocol parsing (L2 through L7), and automated asynchronous threat intelligence enrichment from four premier security providers: \textbf{AbuseIPDB}, \textbf{VirusTotal}, \textbf{IPinfo}, and \textbf{Shodan}.

Designed for security analysts, incident responders, network administrators, and cybersecurity researchers, SentinelShark transforms raw network telemetry into actionable security insights in real time. The software automatically calculates composite threat scores, color-codes suspicious flows, extracts payload hashes (MD5 and SHA256), decodes application protocols, and presents network health through an ergonomic dark-mode user interface.

To ensure wide enterprise applicability, SentinelShark includes both a primary cross-platform Python 3.10+ / PyQt6 application and a standalone native \textbf{C\# .NET 8 / WPF} companion application for Windows environments.

\section{The Problem: Limitations of Legacy Network Analyzers}

Network environments generate vast amounts of high-velocity traffic. When diagnosing potential compromises, data exfiltration, or denial-of-service attempts, security personnel face critical bottlenecks:

\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
  \item \textbf{Absence of Automated Threat Context:} Standard packet analyzers capture raw frames but offer zero correlation with global threat databases. Analysts must copy each public IP address manually into browser tabs to verify reputation.
  \item \textbf{Inefficient Incident Response Triage:} Under high packet volumes, distinguishing benign cloud services from malicious Command-and-Control (C2) servers or Tor exit nodes is challenging without visual risk triage.
  \item \textbf{Binary Dependency Fragility:} Traditional packet sniffers crash or become completely unusable when low-level capture drivers (like Npcap or libpcap) are absent or when users lack administrative privilege.
  \item \textbf{API Rate Limit Exhaustion:} Ad-hoc querying of external threat APIs leads to quota burnout without intelligent rate limiting, local TTL caching, and exponential backoff handling.
\end{itemize}

\section{Proposed Solution \& Core Innovations}

SentinelShark resolves these operational challenges through three architectural pillars:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5cm, label=\textbf{\arabic*.}]
  \item \textbf{Resilient Zero-Friction Capture Architecture:} The engine searches for installed TShark binaries, seamlessly degrades to PyShark, and includes an internal high-fidelity Mock Traffic Generator (30--70 packets/sec). This ensures that researchers can test and demonstrate detection workflows without special hardware or elevated privileges.
  \item \textbf{Asynchronous Non-Blocking Threat Intelligence Pipeline:} External threat lookups run on non-blocking worker threads managed by \texttt{qasync} and \texttt{httpx}. An in-memory \texttt{TTLCache} with LRU eviction prevents duplicate requests, and an exponential backoff engine protects API quotas against HTTP 429 limits.
  \item \textbf{Ergonomic Visual Triage Interface:} Built with a Figma-inspired dark cyber design, SentinelShark visually categorizes every flow using color-coded severity tiers (Safe Green, Warning Amber, Critical Crimson), real-time throughput sparklines, collapsible telemetry cards, and multi-layer protocol decoders.
\end{enumerate}

