\section{Detailed Functional Requirements}

The functional requirements cover the entire operational lifecycle of SentinelShark, from hardware interface binding to multi-feed threat correlation and visualization. These requirements are formalized in Table~\ref{tab:fr}.

\begin{longtable}{clp{11.5cm}}
\caption{Comprehensive Functional Requirements (FR-01 through FR-20)}
\label{tab:fr} \\
\toprule
\textbf{ID} & \textbf{Subsystem} & \textbf{Detailed Requirement Specification} \\
\midrule
\endfirsthead
\toprule
\textbf{ID} & \textbf{Subsystem} & \textbf{Detailed Requirement Specification} \\
\midrule
\endhead
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{r}{\small\textit{Continued on next page}} \\
\endfoot
\bottomrule
\endlastfoot
FR-01 & Live Sniffing & Continuous packet capture from selected host adapter via line-buffered TShark JSON subprocess. \\
FR-02 & Mock Generator & Generate synthetic DNS, HTTP, SSH, and TCP traffic flows at 30--70 packets/sec when TShark is absent. \\
FR-03 & PCAP Import & Load existing .pcap/.pcapng files with dialog prompting user for automated threat intelligence enrichment. \\
FR-04 & PCAP Export & Serialize and save in-memory captured packets into standard libpcap binary files. \\
FR-05 & Protocol Dissection & Parse OSI layers L2 through L7 and generate hierarchical dissection tree nodes. \\
FR-06 & Hex Byte Viewer & Render raw packet payloads in aligned 16-byte offset, hex representation, and printable ASCII. \\
FR-07 & Payload Hashing & Calculate MD5 (32-character) and SHA256 (64-character) cryptographic checksums for packet payloads. \\
FR-08 & Threat Enrichment & Asynchronously query AbuseIPDB, VirusTotal, IPinfo, and Shodan for all observed public IPs. \\
FR-09 & Smart IP Filtering & Identify and bypass RFC 1918, 127.0.0.0/8, 169.254.0.0/16, and multicast ranges from API lookups. \\
FR-10 & In-Memory TTL Cache & Cache query responses in memory for 24 hours with LRU eviction and completeness checks. \\
FR-11 & Request Rate Limiter & Enforce strict inter-request delay ($60 / \text{max\_req\_per\_min}$) across all asynchronous lookups. \\
FR-12 & HTTP 429 Backoff & Implement exponential backoff (starting at 1s, doubling up to 60s) upon encountering HTTP 429 limits. \\
FR-13 & BPF Sanitization & Automatically translate user-friendly shortcuts (\texttt{http}, \texttt{web}, \texttt{ssl}, \texttt{dns}) into valid BPF syntax. \\
FR-14 & Real-Time Filter & Dynamically filter packet table rows by IP, port, protocol, or info text as user types. \\
FR-15 & Visual Color Triage & Color-code table rows based on computed threat risk: Safe (Green), Warning (Amber), Threat (Red). \\
FR-16 & Adapter Selector & Present available host adapters with real-time throughput sparklines and KB/s rate previews. \\
FR-17 & Credential Modal & Configure and securely persist API credentials in \texttt{.env} with password-masked input fields. \\
FR-18 & Telemetry Dashboard & Render live statistics: total packet count, byte volume, protocol proportions, and threat counters. \\
FR-19 & Shodan Fallback & Automatically failover to free Shodan InternetDB if API key is missing or HTTP 403 occurs. \\
FR-20 & Permission Advisor & Detect capture permission denials on macOS/Windows and guide users with elevation prompts. \\
\end{longtable}

\section{Non-Functional Requirements \& Performance Targets}

To ensure commercial-grade reliability during active cyber incidents, SentinelShark establishes rigorous non-functional requirements (NFRs) across four critical operational dimensions:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5cm, label=\textbf{\arabic*.}]
  \item \textbf{Throughput \& Real-Time Responsiveness:} The system must ingest live traffic exceeding 500 packets per second while maintaining a fluid 60 frames-per-second GUI repaint cycle. Under continuous high packet rates, CPU utilization is constrained by batching signal emissions every 20 packets (or 40\,ms) and disabling widget table updates during insert bursts.
  \item \textbf{Asynchronous I/O Isolation:} The PyQt6 main GUI thread must remain completely decoupled from network latency. All HTTP requests to external threat intelligence providers (AbuseIPDB, VirusTotal, IPinfo, Shodan) execute on isolated \texttt{httpx} asynchronous worker loops managed by \texttt{qasync}.
  \item \textbf{Memory Footprint \& Eviction Safety:} To prevent memory exhaustion during long-running capture sessions, the in-memory threat cache enforces a strict upper ceiling of 2,000 unique IP entries using a Least-Recently-Used (LRU) eviction algorithm.
  \item \textbf{Secret Isolation \& Zero-Leakage Security:} User API keys and authentication tokens must never be written to committed version-controlled files. All secrets reside exclusively inside a git-ignored \texttt{.env} configuration file.
\end{enumerate}

These engineering constraints and service-level objectives are formally tabulated in Table~\ref{tab:nfr}.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Non-Functional Requirements and Operational Constraints}
\label{tab:nfr}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{clX}
\toprule
\textbf{ID} & \textbf{Category} & \textbf{Technical Specification / Quality Constraint} \\
\midrule
NFR-01 & GUI Responsiveness & Packet table and charts must sustain $>$500 pkts/s without dropping GUI frames (60 FPS). \\
NFR-02 & Asynchronous I/O & External HTTP requests must execute in async coroutines without blocking the Qt event loop. \\
NFR-03 & UI Batch Rendering & Incoming packets must be emitted in batches of 20 items or 40\,ms to minimize repaint cycles. \\
NFR-04 & Failover Resilience & Packet capture must gracefully degrade across TShark $\to$ PyShark $\to$ Mock Mode without crashing. \\
NFR-05 & Secret Security & API keys must reside exclusively in git-ignored \texttt{.env} files and never in \texttt{config.json}. \\
NFR-06 & Memory Ceiling & Threat cache must strictly enforce a maximum memory capacity of 2,000 entries using LRU eviction. \\
NFR-07 & OS Portability & The Python application must run identically on macOS 12+ and Windows 10/11 without modification. \\
NFR-08 & Architectural Decoupling & All UI components must communicate with Core and Service layers strictly via PyQt signals. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

