\section{Packet Capture Lifecycle \& Multi-Tier Fallback}

The packet capture subsystem is designed to ensure zero operational failure regardless of host environmental constraints. When the user initiates packet sniffing, the capture engine executes a 4-tier evaluation sequence:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5cm, label=\textbf{\arabic*.}]
  \item \textbf{Mock Mode Check:} If the user explicitly enabled Mock Mode (via the toolbar toggle or \texttt{MOCK\_MODE=true} environment variable), the engine immediately launches \texttt{run\_mock\_capture()}, generating realistic synthetic network flows (30--70 packets/sec).
  \item \textbf{Offline PCAP File Capture:} If a saved capture file was opened, the engine delegates decoding to PyShark's \texttt{FileCapture} stream.
  \item \textbf{Direct Line-Buffered TShark Subprocess:} In live mode, the engine invokes a native subprocess (\texttt{tshark -l -n -i <interface> -T json -x}) streaming line-delimited JSON packet objects directly over stdout pipes. This direct pipe achieves maximum throughput ($>$500 pkts/s).
  \item \textbf{Automatic Fallback Chain:} If direct TShark subprocess initialization fails due to driver permission errors or binary path issues, the system automatically falls back to \texttt{PyShark.LiveCapture}. If that also fails, the engine seamlessly activates the Mock Traffic Generator and notifies the user with diagnostic remediation steps.
\end{enumerate}

Figure~\ref{fig:capture_flow} illustrates the decision tree and event emission lifecycle of the capture engine.

