\section{Threat Intelligence Pipeline Architecture}

The asynchronous threat intelligence pipeline converts raw IP addresses into enriched threat intelligence profiles through an orchestrated multi-stage lifecycle:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5cm, label=\textbf{\arabic*.}]
  \item \textbf{IP Address Extraction \& Validation:} When a packet is parsed, \texttt{src\_ip} and \texttt{dst\_ip} are checked against Python's \texttt{ipaddress} module via \texttt{is\_public\_ip()}. Private, loopback, multicast, and link-local addresses are filtered out immediately.
  \item \textbf{Fast-Path Cache Inspection:} The IP is queried against the in-memory \texttt{ThreatCache}. On a cache hit, a \texttt{threat\_resolved} signal is emitted instantly, avoiding network calls.
  \item \textbf{Queue Deduplication \& Pacing:} Uncached public IPs are submitted to \texttt{ThreatIntelQueueManager}. If an IP is already pending or in-progress, the duplicate request is discarded. The queue enforces a strict inter-request pacing delay ($60 / \text{max\_requests\_per\_minute}$) to protect rate limits.
  \item \textbf{Parallel Multi-Provider Execution:} Four asynchronous coroutines are spawned concurrently using \texttt{asyncio.gather()} to query AbuseIPDB, VirusTotal, IPinfo, and Shodan.
  \item \textbf{Exponential 429 Backoff:} If any provider returns an HTTP 429 Rate Limited status, the worker enters an exponential backoff loop (starting at 1.0s, doubling up to 60.0s max) before re-queueing the target IP.
  \item \textbf{Cache Storage \& Signal Broadcast:} Completed threat profiles are stored in \texttt{ThreatCache} (TTL: 24 hours) and broadcast via \texttt{threat\_resolved(ip, data)} to update the \texttt{PacketTable} and \texttt{StatsPanel}.
\end{enumerate}

Figure~\ref{fig:threat_flow} details the entire asynchronous data flow from frame ingestion to GUI presentation.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[node distance=0.6cm and 2.0cm]

