50--89 & High Risk & Amber Warning & \texttt{\#78350F} & \texttt{\#FEF3C7} & Known malware host / CVEs \\
1--49 & Low Suspicion & Yellow Tint & \texttt{\#713F12} & \texttt{\#FEF08A} & Port scanning history \\
0 (Public) & Safe Verified & Green Tint & \texttt{\#064E3B} & \texttt{\#D1FAE5} & Google DNS (8.8.8.8) \\
Private IP & Internal LAN & Neutral Slate & Default & Default & 192.168.1.1, 10.0.0.1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{BPF Filter Sanitization}

When capturing high-volume network traffic, applying low-level Berkeley Packet Filter (BPF) capture filters directly at the kernel driver layer is essential to avoid user-space buffer bloat. However, raw BPF syntax can be complex and error-prone for analysts. SentinelShark includes a built-in BPF sanitizer (\texttt{sanitize\_bpf\_filter}) that normalizes intuitive protocol shortcuts into valid kernel-level BPF filter expressions.

\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
  \item \textbf{Protocol Aliasing:} Shortcuts such as \texttt{http}, \texttt{web}, \texttt{https}, \texttt{ssl}, \texttt{tls}, \texttt{dns}, and \texttt{ssh} are automatically mapped to their respective TCP/UDP port filter expressions.
  \item \textbf{Host Wrapping:} Raw IPv4 addresses (such as \texttt{8.8.8.8}) are detected via regular expression and wrapped into standard \texttt{host 8.8.8.8} expressions.
  \item \textbf{Pass-Through Integrity:} Valid, pre-formatted BPF syntax (such as \texttt{tcp port 80 and host 192.168.1.1}) passes through untouched.
\end{itemize}

Table~\ref{tab:bpf} summarizes the translation rules applied by the sanitizer.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{BPF Shortcut Translation Engine}
\label{tab:bpf}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
\textbf{User Filter Input} & \textbf{Translated BPF Expression} & \textbf{Traffic Scope} \\
\midrule
\texttt{http} or \texttt{web} & \texttt{tcp port 80 or tcp port 8080} & Standard unencrypted web \\
\texttt{https} or \texttt{ssl} or \texttt{tls} & \texttt{tcp port 443} & Encrypted HTTPS sessions \\
\texttt{dns} & \texttt{port 53} & UDP and TCP domain queries \\
\texttt{ssh} & \texttt{tcp port 22} & Secure Shell connections \\
\texttt{icmp} & \texttt{icmp} & ICMP ping and diagnostics \\
\texttt{tcp} / \texttt{udp} & \texttt{tcp} / \texttt{udp} & Full transport layer protocol \\
\texttt{8.8.8.8} (Raw IP) & \texttt{host 8.8.8.8} & Automatically wrapped with \texttt{host} \\
\texttt{tcp port 80} (Valid BPF) & \texttt{tcp port 80} & Passed through unchanged \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Supported Network Protocols}

SentinelShark features protocol dissectors operating across OSI layers L2 through L7. Application-layer dissectors inspect payload signatures and port assignments, decoding DNS queries, HTTP headers, TLS handshakes, and SSH banners. In the telemetry dashboard, each protocol is assigned a distinct visual color tag for rapid visual triage. Table~\ref{tab:protocols} outlines the supported protocol decoders, detection criteria, and color mappings.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Protocol Decoding and Dashboard Color Scheme}
\label{tab:protocols}
\begin{tabular}{lllll}
\toprule
\textbf{Protocol} & \textbf{OSI Layer} & \textbf{Identification Rule} & \textbf{Dashboard Color} & \textbf{Default Port} \\
\midrule
DNS & L7 Application & \texttt{dns} layer present & Blue (\texttt{\#60A5FA}) & Port 53 \\
HTTP & L7 Application & \texttt{http} layer present & Amber (\texttt{\#FBBF24}) & Port 80, 8080 \\
HTTPS / TLS & L7 Application & \texttt{tls} layer present & Emerald (\texttt{\#34D399}) & Port 443 \\
SSH & L7 Application & \texttt{ssh} layer present & Rose (\texttt{\#F87171}) & Port 22 \\
QUIC & L4--L7 Hybrid & \texttt{quic} layer present & Teal (\texttt{\#2DD4BF}) & Port 443 (UDP) \\
ICMP / ICMPv6 & L3 Network & \texttt{icmp}/\texttt{icmpv6} layer & Sky Blue (\texttt{\#38BDF8}) & N/A \\
TCP (Generic) & L4 Transport & \texttt{tcp} without app layer & Slate (\texttt{\#94A3B8}) & Variable \\
UDP (Generic) & L4 Transport & \texttt{udp} without app layer & Purple (\texttt{\#A78BFA}) & Variable \\
ARP & L2 Data Link & \texttt{arp} layer present & Indigo (\texttt{\#818CF8}) & N/A \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

