\section{Control Toolbar \& Interactive Controls}

The top toolbar acts as the operational command center:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
  \item \textbf{Adapter Selector Combo:} Displays host interfaces with real-time throughput status.
  \item \textbf{BPF Filter LineEdit:} Accepts raw BPF syntax or intuitive shortcuts (e.g., \texttt{http}, \texttt{ssl}, \texttt{dns}).
  \item \textbf{Start / Stop Capture Buttons:} Neon Green (\texttt{\#22C55E}) for Start, Crimson (\texttt{\#EF4444}) for Stop.
  \item \textbf{Clear Session Button:} Resets table rows, packet counters, and telemetry sparklines.
  \item \textbf{Save Session Button:} Exports active packet buffer to Wireshark-compatible binary \texttt{.pcap}.
  \item \textbf{Mock Toggle Button:} Toggles synthetic traffic generator without restarting the application.
  \item \textbf{API Key Settings Modal Trigger:} Opens dialog to update AbuseIPDB, VT, IPinfo, and Shodan keys.
\end{itemize}

\section{Live Packet Table (\texttt{PacketTable})}

The \texttt{PacketTable} widget renders live traffic using an 8-column virtualized \texttt{QTableWidget}:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
  \item \textbf{Column 0 (No.):} Sequential 1-based integer, right-aligned (width: 60px).
  \item \textbf{Column 1 (Time):} Capture timestamp \texttt{HH:MM:SS.mmm} (width: 100px).
  \item \textbf{Column 2 (Source):} Source IP or MAC address (width: 130px).
  \item \textbf{Column 3 (Destination):} Destination IP or MAC address (width: 130px).
  \item \textbf{Column 4 (Protocol):} Protocol identifier with color tag (width: 80px).
  \item \textbf{Column 5 (Length):} Total byte length on wire (width: 70px).
  \item \textbf{Column 6 (Info):} Detailed Wireshark-style dissection summary (expanding width).
  \item \textbf{Column 7 (Threat Score):} Composite score badge (e.g., \texttt{99\% Risk}, \texttt{0\% Safe}).
\end{itemize}

\section{Live Telemetry \& Statistics Dashboard (\texttt{StatsPanel})}

The right sidebar provides a multi-card real-time telemetry dashboard:
\begin{enumerate}[noitemsep]
  \item \textbf{2$\times$2 Metric Grid:} Displays Total Packets (Cyan), Data Traffic in KB/MB (Teal), Safe Packets (Green), and Threats Detected (Red).
  \item \textbf{Protocol Breakdown Bar:} Visual horizontal distribution bars illustrating top 5 active protocols.
  \item \textbf{Threat Intelligence Queue Monitor:} Real-time connection status badges for AbuseIPDB, VirusTotal, IPinfo, and Shodan, alongside an active queue progress bar.
  \item \textbf{Selected Packet Inspector Card:} Live summary of the currently highlighted packet row.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Protocol Dissection Tree (\texttt{PacketDetailView})}

The \texttt{PacketDetailView} expands packets into a collapsible \texttt{QTreeWidget} hierarchy:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
  \item \textbf{Threat Intelligence Root Node:} Summarizes AbuseIPDB confidence, VirusTotal engine count, IPinfo location, and Shodan CVE vulnerabilities.
  \item \textbf{Frame Layer Node:} Frame number, wire arrival timestamp, and total byte length.
  \item \textbf{Ethernet II Node:} Source/Destination MAC addresses and 16-bit EtherType.
  \item \textbf{Internet Protocol (IPv4/IPv6) Node:} Header length, TTL, protocol type, checksum, and IP addresses.
  \item \textbf{Transport Layer (TCP/UDP) Node:} Source/Destination ports, sequence/acknowledgment numbers, and flags.
  \item \textbf{Application Layer Node:} Protocol-specific decoded fields (HTTP headers, DNS queries, TLS handshakes).
\end{itemize}

\section{Hexadecimal \& ASCII Byte Inspector (\texttt{HexView})}

The \texttt{HexView} widget displays raw frame payloads using a monospace font (\texttt{JetBrains Mono}):
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
  \item Formatted in standard 16-byte rows grouped in two 8-byte blocks with left-aligned hexadecimal offsets.
  \item Printable ASCII characters displayed in the right column (non-printable bytes rendered as dots).
  \item Dedicated badge chips at the bottom displaying calculated \textbf{MD5} and \textbf{SHA256} checksums with one-click clipboard copying.
\end{itemize}

\section{Real-Time Bandwidth Sparkline (\texttt{SparklineWidget})}

The \texttt{SparklineWidget} is a custom \texttt{QPainter} component rendering an antialiased waveform:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
  \item Maintains a rolling circular \texttt{deque} of 30 throughput measurements (sampled every 300\,ms via \texttt{psutil}).
  \item Active traffic renders with a neon cyan gradient fill (\texttt{\#38BDF8}) and an emerald pulsing dot.
  \item Idle adapters render as a muted slate dotted baseline.
\end{itemize}

\section{Network Interface Selection Dialog}

Upon application startup, the \texttt{InterfaceSelectionDialog} scans all host network adapters, displaying:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
  \item Friendly operating system interface labels (e.g. \texttt{Wi-Fi (en0)} or \texttt{Ethernet 2}).
  \item Primary IPv4 and MAC addresses.
  \item Live miniature throughput sparklines updating every 300\,ms.
  \item Real-time KB/s transfer rates allowing users to easily spot the active network adapter.
\end{itemize}

\section{API Key Configuration Dialog}

The \texttt{APISettingsDialog} allows users to manage credentials and performance parameters:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
  \item Password-masked fields for AbuseIPDB, VirusTotal, IPinfo, and Shodan API keys.
  \item Cache TTL duration configuration (default: 24 hours).
  \item Global API request pacing slider (default: 30 requests/minute).
  \item Immediate in-memory cache purge and queue re-synchronization on save.
\end{itemize}

