% ============================================================
%  CHAPTER 6: CONFIGURATION & SECRET MANAGEMENT
% ============================================================
\chapter{Configuration \& Secret Management}

\section{Two-File Configuration Architecture}

To ensure complete credential security while maintaining flexible application defaults, SentinelShark employs a two-tier configuration architecture:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
  \item \textbf{\texttt{.env} File (Private Secrets):} Contains sensitive API credentials and local machine overrides. This file is automatically excluded via \texttt{.gitignore} and never committed to source repositories.
  \item \textbf{\texttt{config.json} File (Shared Defaults):} Contains general application settings (cache expiration, rate limits, UI preferences) that are safe to commit.
\end{itemize}

\section{Automatic Legacy Configuration Migration}

Upon startup, the \texttt{Config} singleton automatically inspects \texttt{config.json}. If legacy API credentials are detected, the system automatically migrates them into the local \texttt{.env} file and deletes the secret keys from \texttt{config.json}, ensuring that users never accidentally commit private credentials to git.

\section{Configuration Parameter Reference}

Table~\ref{tab:config} lists all supported configuration parameters, data types, default values, and storage targets.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Complete Configuration Options}
\label{tab:config}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{llXll}
\toprule
\textbf{Option Key} & \textbf{Type} & \textbf{Description} & \textbf{Default} & \textbf{Storage} \\
\midrule
\texttt{ABUSEIPDB\_API\_KEY} & string & Authentication token for AbuseIPDB v2 API & \textit{empty} & \texttt{.env} \\
\texttt{VIRUSTOTAL\_API\_KEY} & string & Authentication key for VirusTotal v3 API & \textit{empty} & \texttt{.env} \\
\texttt{IPINFO\_API\_KEY} & string & Access token for IPinfo geolocation API & \textit{empty} & \texttt{.env} \\
\texttt{SHODAN\_API\_KEY} & string & API key for Shodan Host query API & \textit{empty} & \texttt{.env} \\
\texttt{DEFAULT\_INTERFACE} & string & Default capture adapter name & \texttt{auto} & \texttt{.env} \\
\texttt{MOCK\_MODE} & bool & Force simulated traffic generation & \texttt{false} & \texttt{.env} \\
\texttt{AUTO\_SCROLL} & bool & Automatically scroll table to newest packet & \texttt{true} & \texttt{.env} \\
\texttt{TSHARK\_PATH} & string & Custom path to TShark binary executable & \textit{empty} & \texttt{.env} \\
\texttt{CACHE\_TTL\_HOURS} & int & In-memory threat cache retention in hours & \texttt{24} & Both \\
\texttt{MAX\_REQ\_PER\_MINUTE} & int & Global API query pacing rate limit & \texttt{30} & Both \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

\section{TShark Auto-Discovery Hierarchy}

When initializing live capture, SentinelShark automatically scans the host environment in the following strict search order:
\begin{enumerate}[noitemsep]
  \item Path defined in \texttt{config.tshark\_path} (if file exists on disk).
  \item Host system \texttt{PATH} environment variable via \texttt{shutil.which("tshark")}.
  \item Standard macOS bundle: \texttt{/Applications/Wireshark.app/Contents/MacOS/tshark}.
  \item Standard Unix paths: \texttt{/usr/local/bin/tshark}, \texttt{/opt/homebrew/bin/tshark}, and \texttt{/usr/bin/tshark}.
  \item Standard Windows paths: \texttt{C:\textbackslash Program Files\textbackslash Wireshark\textbackslash tshark.exe}.
\end{enumerate}

% ============================================================
%  CHAPTER 7: TESTING & QUALITY ASSURANCE
% ============================================================
\chapter{Testing \& Quality Assurance}

\section{Testing Philosophy \& Verification Methodology}

The testing strategy of SentinelShark is built upon the core principle of **hermetic determinism**: all unit tests must execute with zero external network connectivity, zero reliance on live capture drivers, and zero credential dependencies. This ensures that the test suite runs reliably in continuous integration (CI) environments, on developer machines without root privileges, and in offline security testing labs.

\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
  \item \textbf{Isolated Unit Fixtures:} Test cases instantiate fresh, unshared instances of \texttt{ThreatCache}, \texttt{Config}, and \texttt{PacketTable} to prevent state leakage across test boundaries.
  \item \textbf{Deterministic Mock Ingestion:} Dissectors and formatters are tested against hardcoded libpcap hex strings and JSON fixtures representing real-world attack vectors (e.g., SYN flood packets, Tor relay handshakes).
  \item \textbf{Boundary Value Coverage:} Tests explicitly assert RFC 1918 edge cases (e.g., \texttt{10.255.255.255}, \texttt{172.31.255.255}, \texttt{192.168.255.255}), link-local addresses (\texttt{169.254.0.1}), and loopback ranges (\texttt{127.0.0.1}, \texttt{::1}).
\end{itemize}

\section{Detailed Unit Test Case Specifications}

The automated test suite is implemented in \texttt{tests/test\_sentinelshark.py} (309 lines of code) using Python's standard \texttt{unittest} library. Table~\ref{tab:tests} specifies all 13 test methods, the target subsystem tested, the invariant validated, and the verification status.

