\section{Cross-Platform Engineering Analysis}

To ensure seamless operation across both Unix (macOS/Linux) and Windows environments, SentinelShark resolves several fundamental operating system disparities:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
  \item \textbf{Event Loop Disparity:} On Windows, \texttt{asyncio} defaults to \texttt{ProactorEventLoop}, which can conflict with child subprocess pipes. SentinelShark explicitly enforces \texttt{WindowsSelectorEventLoopPolicy} on Windows startup.
  \item \textbf{Capture Interface Mapping:} On macOS/Linux, interfaces are referenced by simple names (\texttt{en0}, \texttt{eth0}). On Windows, TShark exposes raw NPF GUIDs (\texttt{\textbackslash Device\textbackslash NPF\_\{...\}}). SentinelShark's \texttt{NetworkInterfaceMapper} automatically queries the Windows Registry (\texttt{HKLM\textbackslash SYSTEM\textbackslash CurrentControlSet\textbackslash Control\textbackslash Network}) to resolve GUIDs to friendly names (e.g. \texttt{Wi-Fi} or \texttt{Ethernet}).
\end{itemize}

Table~\ref{tab:compat} provides the complete platform compatibility matrix.

